.TH LONGWIRE-SECURITY 7 "October 2026" "longwire 0.5.8" "longwire manual"
.SH NAME
longwire-security \- what longwire protects, what it does not, and how far it has been checked
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B longwire
is designed to be hard to attack.
Its key exchange is built so that a quantum computer would have to break ML-KEM-768 as well as X25519 to read traffic recorded today.
It runs without root, lets in only the nodes you picked up or that a member of your line vouched for (or the line's operator signed for), and was attacked on purpose before release, with the flaws found fixed.
.PP
It is
.B not
"ultra safe", and should not be called that.
It has had no independent security review.
Its handshake is a new combination of standard parts that has not been formally analysed.
The list of what has not been checked is below.
.SH "WHAT IT PROTECTS"
.TP
.B Confidentiality and integrity
Every frame is sealed with ChaCha20-Poly1305 (256-bit keys).
A packet that fails authentication is dropped; a replayed packet is dropped.
.TP
.B Forward secrecy
The traffic keys are made fresh by each handshake, renewed every two minutes, and erased.
.TP
.B Admission
The first call from a number asks you, under the default answer policy.
A machine joins a line only if you picked it up, or a member vouched for it; on a line with an operator, only the operator's signature admits.
Every member is then trusted like a host on your LAN.
.TP
.B Identity
Each end proves it holds the key that matches its number.
After the first call the key is pinned: a different key behind the same number drops the call and warns
.RB ( "KEY CHANGED" ).
.TP
.B The safety phrase
Both ends show the same six words, derived from both keys.
The person called sees them on the ring, the caller when the call connects: compare them on another channel at once, and hang up if they differ.
A match is good evidence that nobody is between you.
The phrase is 48 bits with no commitment, so a switchboard that is itself the attacker has a search to do, of the order of 2^24 key pairs, but not an impossible one.
.TP
.B A hostile switchboard
It never has a frame, a seed or a private key, and it cannot make you accept a call.
On a first call you have never made, it can give a node the wrong key for a number; once a key is pinned, or the phrase compared, it cannot.
.TP
.B Floods
A packet that does not prove it knows the receiver's key is dropped before any costly work.
.SH "QUANTUM-SAFE: WHICH PARTS"
.TP
.B Yes
Confidentiality of recorded traffic, forward secrecy, hiding who is calling, and authenticating the two ends of the handshake.
The handshake combines X25519 with ML-KEM-768 (FIPS 203); an attacker must break both.
The cipher's 256-bit keys keep 128 bits against the best known quantum attack.
.TP
.B No
The Ed25519 signatures that vouch for line membership (admits, evictions, an operator's word).
A future quantum computer could forge them.
A forged admit would get a stranger let in to a line without a prompt: it would be a member, on your LAN, able to do what any LAN host can, though not to read sessions between other members.
A post-quantum signature is a later option.
.SH "HOW IT HAS BEEN CHECKED"
A test suite that builds real networks with simulated NATs;
an attack on the switchboard by three readers, which found and fixed real flaws;
a fuzzer that runs the engine under TCC's bounds checker;
and a comparison of X25519, BLAKE2b, ChaCha20-Poly1305 and Ed25519, as built, with independent implementations.
.SH "WHAT HAS NOT BEEN CHECKED"
No independent review or audit.
No formal proof of the handshake.
ML-KEM has not been compared with a second implementation.
Constant-time behaviour, as the code is compiled, is unmeasured.
The engine is written in C.
A real tap device, real routers, the wide-area internet, the services under a live systemd and a clean install from the packages have not been tried by the author.
.SH "WHAT IT DOES NOT PROTECT"
Traffic analysis: that two addresses talk, and when.
A compromised endpoint.
A line member you should not trust: a call is a cable into your LAN.
What a switchboard learns: that you are online, your public address, who rings whom, and the names you gave it.
Floods of plain traffic.
Loss of the seed: it is your number.
.SH "USING IT SAFELY"
Compare the safety phrase on a first call.
Keep the answer policy at
.B ask
or
.BR known .
Back up the seed as 33 words
.RB ( "longwire key export" ).
Run your own switchboard and hand out tokens.
Set a token with
.BR "longwire profile token" ,
not on a command line.
Vouch for few machines.
Evict a lost device.
Put only trusted people in the group
.BR longwire .
Keep the packages and the scripts updated.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR longwire (1),
.BR longwire-switchboard (1),
.BR longwire-profiles (5)
.PP
